    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d585">

                <pb facs="0728" n="714" xml:id="pg714"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.8820/1206: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Norweb</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-05-14T18:00:00-05:00">May 14, 1943—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 10:21 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>697. Reference Embassy’s telegram No. 680, May 11, 7 p.m.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d585-f1"/> and previous communications regarding Peruvian ships in
                    United States trade. The Finance Minister and the Embassy have received notice
                    from Grace and Company that the War Shipping Administration insists the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ucayali</hi> load cargo for New Orleans and gives the
                    impression the ship is more or less prohibited from going to San Francisco.</p>
                <p>Our original agreement was the two ships would go to the West Coast. The Finance
                    Minister has assumed a responsible attitude but earnestly requests that the
                    ships be authorized as usual to load for San Francisco until full cargoes for
                    Peru are available there. Thereafter he will send them to New Orleans to
                    cooperate as closely as possible with the United States.</p>
                <p>This request seems reasonable and I wish to recommend it for the Department’s
                    most serious consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norweb</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
